Let me write /workspace/solution.py with the CUDA implementation described, plus quick fallback. Then push to box and test correctness & timing manually before running full eval.

Kernel details:

```cpp
#include <torch/extension.h>
#include <cuda_runtime.h>

#define FULL_MASK 0xffffffffu

__inline__ __device__ float warp_sum(float v) {
  v += __shfl_xor_sync(FULL_MASK, v, 16);
  v += __shfl_xor_sync(FULL_MASK, v, 8);
  v += __shfl_xor_sync(FULL_MASK, v, 4);
  v += __shfl_xor_sync(FULL_MASK, v, 2);
  v += __shfl_xor_sync(FULL_MASK, v, 1);
  return v;
}

extern "C" __global__ void row_partials(const float* __restrict__ x, float* __restrict__ partials, long D, int nblocks) {
  // grid: (nblocks, B), block: (32, nwarps)
  const int row = blockIdx.y;
  const float4* xr = reinterpret_cast<const float4*>(x + (long)row * D);
  const long n4 = D >> 2;
  float s = 0.f, sq = 0.f;
  for (long i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y; i < n4; i += (long)gridDim.x * blockDim.y) {
    float4 v = xr[i];
    s += v.x + v.y + v.z + v.w;
    sq += v.x*v.x + v.y*v.y + v.z*v.z + v.w*v.w;
  }
  s = warp_sum(s); sq = warp_sum(sq);
  __shared__ float sh_s[32]; // nwarps max 32
  __shared__ float sh_q[32];
  const int lane = threadIdx.x, warp = threadIdx.y;
  const int nw = blockDim.y;
  if (lane == 0) { sh_s[warp] = s; sh_q[warp] = sq; }
  __syncthreads();
  if (warp == 0) {
    float a = (lane < nw) ? sh_s[lane] : 0.f;
    float b = (lane < nw) ? sh_q[lane] : 0.f;
    a = warp_sum(a); b = warp_sum(b);
    if (lane == 0) {
      partials[row * nblocks + blockIdx.x] = a;
      partials[row * nblocks + nblocks + blockIdx.x] = b;
    }
  }
}
```

Kernel2: 

```cpp
extern "C" __global__ void ln_apply(const float* __restrict__ x, const float* __restrict__ w, const float* __restrict__ b,
                                    float* __restrict__ y, const float* __restrict__ partials, long D, int nblocks, float eps) {
  const int row = blockIdx.y;
  // reduce partials: nblocks up to 4096 → loop
  float s = 0.f, q = 0.f;
  for (int i = threadIdx.x; i < nblocks; i += blockDim.x) {
    s += partials[row * nblocks + i];
    q += partials[row * nblocks + nblocks + i];
  }
  // block reduce over blockDim.x threads (one warp config: blockDim.x=32*nw?)
  ...
  __shared__ float sh_mean, sh_rstd;
  if (tid==0) { float mean = s / D; float var = q / D - mean*mean; sh_mean=mean; sh_rstd = rsqrtf(var + eps); }
  __syncthreads();
  float mean = sh_mean, rstd = sh_rstd;
  const float4* xr = ...; float4* yr = ...;
  const float4* wr = (const float4*)w; const float4* br = (const float4*)b;
  for (long i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y; i < n4; i += stride) {
    float4 v = xr[i], wv = wr[i], bv = br[i];
    float4 o;
    o.x = (v.x - mean) * rstd * wv.x + bv.x; ...
    yr[i] = o;
  }
}
```

Grid for kernel2: B × nb2 blocks; with nb2 = 64: 1024 blocks, each thread ~1024 float4 → strided loop 8 iterations with 128 threads/block (32×4)? Let me choose block = 128 threads (4 warps), nb2 = 64 blocks/row: each block covers 65536/64 = 1024 float4 = 8 per thread. Good ILP.

But wait, should kernel2's loop be grid-stride with coalescing across blocks? index i = blockIdx.x * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y: consecutive threadIdx.y → consecutive i: coalesced within warp rows. Block covers contiguous chunk if I use block-contiguous mapping instead: i in [blockIdx.x*chunk, (blockIdx.x+1)*chunk) with thread stride. Strided-across-grid is also fine for coalescing at warp granularity. Keep grid-stride.

Kernel1 config: nb1 blocks per row; each thread accumulates many elements → at D4=1,048,576 float4s, nb1=128 blocks × 128 threads = 16384 threads per row ×16 rows = 262,144 threads. Each thread 64 float4 loads. Total blocks 2048. Occupancy-wise H100 has 132 SMs; 2048 blocks of 128 threads → 16 waves approx; fine.

Reduce partials in kernel2: nb1=128 → each thread of first warp loads 4 values. Cheap.

Alternatively kernel1 nb1 = 64. Tune later.

Launch overhead: 2 kernels ≈ 6-10µs total CPU, hidden under L2-clear kernel as analyzed.

Also for determinism of correctness vs reference: differences ~1e-6, tolerance 1e-4. 

Edge: var could be tiny negative → rsqrtf(var+eps) with var+eps ≥ ~eps > 0 fine.

One important thing: D is passed long; n4 = D/4 exact (D=4194304 divisible by 4). Assert on host.

Host function:

```cpp
torch::Tensor ln_forward(torch::Tensor x, torch::Tensor weight, torch::Tensor bias, double eps) {
  auto y = torch::empty_like(x);
  long D = weight.numel();
  long B = x.numel() / D;
  int nb1 = 128, nb2 = 64;
  auto partials = torch::empty({B * nb1 * 2}, x.options());
  dim3 g1(nb1, B), b1(32, 4);
  row_partials<<<g1, b1>>>(...);
  dim3 g2(nb2, B), b2(32, 4);
  ln_apply<<<g2, b2>>>(...);
  return y;
}
```

Threads 32×4=128.

