Now about matching speed: F.layer_norm as fallback gives speedup ~1.0.

Let me also reconsider single-kernel approach with cooperative groups grid sync — fewer launches, avoids partials round trip. complexity higher; try v1 two-kernel first, measure, then optimize (maybe merge into one kernel with cooperative launch; or use PDL?).

Actually, another idea for kernel1+kernel2 fusion: use one kernel where each block computes partials, atomicAdd to global accumulators, then last-block-per-row (atomic counter) computes mean/rstd and... but then all blocks need to re-read x — the blocks that finished early already read x once. Classic split-K with semaphore: blocks do: 1) read x chunk, accumulate; 2) atomic add partial; 3) if last block for row: compute mean/rstd to global; 4) all blocks then spin-wait on flag, then re-read x chunk and write y. This reads x twice per block but from L2 possibly (x is 64MB > 50MB L2, partial). The spin-wait approach risks deadlock if blocks not co-resident... with grid sized to fit (persistent-CTA style), safe. This is essentially cooperative groups. Two-kernel is simpler and safe; overhead only ~3µs. Start there.

Also alternative single-pass: persistent kernel with 132*? blocks where each block handles one row entirely? Only 16 rows → 16 blocks → underutilized. No.

What about splitting rows across many blocks and using fp32 atomicAdd directly for sum and sumsq (no partials buffer, deterministic-ish? atomics nondeterministic order but correctness tolerance 1e-4 fine). Kernel1 with atomicAdd to 2*B floats: contention: 2048 blocks × 2 atomics each on 32 addresses → 4096 atomics, serialized per address ~ 128 per address — fine, ~ few µs. Then kernel2 same. This reduces partial buffer traffic (negligible anyway).

